text:
1.2 Work Tools
Regardless of profile, a web programmer typically works with the following set of tools:

Computer and operating system: macOS, Windows or Linux;
Browser: Chrome, Firefox, Safari or Edge, to test the result of the work;
Text editor or IDE: Visual Studio Code, Sublime Text or a more complete IDE, such as WebStorm;
Terminal: the command line on macOS or Linux, or PowerShell or Git Bash on Windows;
Design software: for prototypes and interfaces, with Figma as the market’s reference tool (Mapsoft, n.d.).

1.3 HTML and CSS: the Basic Building Blocks
HTML and CSS are the fundamental building blocks of any web page: HTML structures the content and CSS defines its visual presentation. A frontend programmer should master, at minimum:

The basic and semantic HTML5 tags;
The fundamentals of CSS: selectors, properties and the box model;
Alignment and grids in CSS, using Flexbox and CSS Grid;
Media queries and responsive design, so the page works across different screen sizes;
Simple animations and transitions.

1.5 What an Entry-Level Frontend Developer Should Know
Summarising what is expected of a frontend programmer at the start of their career:

Build web layouts with HTML and CSS;
Know JavaScript and work with the DOM (Document Object Model);
Build small, interactive frontend applications;
Be able to connect those applications to external APIs;
Work with Git and use version control;
Install and manage small, real projects already in production.

3.2 The Project: Contraponto Magazine
Throughout this and the following chapters, the code examples follow the same project, so that each new concept is seen applied to a real problem, instead of disconnected, standalone examples.
Rita Ferreira has run Contraponto, a cultural print magazine, for over ten years, and now wants a digital version: a site where the magazine’s articles are available online, organised by category, with room for readers to comment. Tiago Correia, a final-year Communication and Multimedia student, takes on the challenge of building that site over the semester, applying what he learns in this course.
Before writing the first line of code, Tiago proposes a simple sketch to Rita of the data the site will need to store:

Article: each published piece of content (title, summary, text, author, category, publication date);
Author: who writes the magazine’s articles;
Category: the topic of each article, for example Culture, Technology or Music;
Comment: what readers write about an article.

This sketch is not yet a database, only a map of the problem: the implementation, chapter by chapter, starts with the simplest parts, and only adds new complexity as Tiago learns new tools of the language, from the arrays seen in Section 3.8 to the database tables from Section 8.1 onward.

3.4 The PHP Language
PHP (Hypertext Preprocessor) is a scripting language that runs on the server side. Unlike HTML and JavaScript, which the browser receives and interprets directly, PHP code is executed on the server, and the client only receives the result of that processing, typically HTML. This means PHP code is never visible to whoever visits the site, even if they view the page’s source code in the browser: everything the client receives is already the result of PHP’s execution, never the PHP code itself.
A PHP code section always starts with &lt;?php and can be closed with ?&gt;, although, in files that contain only PHP, it is common to omit the closing tag, to avoid accidental whitespace after it. PHP can be inserted in the middle of HTML, mixing both languages in the same file, entering and leaving PHP mode as many times as needed:
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Hello!&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;?php
    echo "&lt;p&gt;It is now " . date("H:i") . "&lt;/p&gt;";
  ?&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;This text is no longer PHP, it is plain HTML.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;?php
    $visitas = 128;
    echo "&lt;p&gt;This site has been visited $visitas times.&lt;/p&gt;";
  ?&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
PHP supports two programming styles: procedural, a sequence of instructions and function calls, which is what this book follows as its base, and object-oriented, in which data and behaviour are grouped into objects, created with new and manipulated through methods, called with an arrow (-&gt;). PHP itself relies on objects in some of its own internal tools, such as the DateTime class, seen in Section 5.2; in those cases, only the syntax needed to use them is introduced, without yet needing to know how to build objects of one’s own.

3.5 Basic Syntax
Every PHP statement ends with a semicolon (;); forgetting it is one of the most common mistakes made by those starting to program in PHP, and it typically produces a syntax error (parse error) that prevents the page from loading. Single-line comments are made with //, and multi-line ones with /* ... */:
&lt;?php
// This single-line comment does not appear on the page

/* This comment
   can span
   several lines */

echo "Hello, World!";
To display a value, the echo command is used, which can take several values separated by commas:
&lt;?php
echo "Hello, World!";
echo "Name: ", "John", ", Age: ", 25;

3.8 Arrays
Tiago starts with the most visible part of the Contraponto site: the list of published articles. A first attempt, perhaps the most obvious for someone starting to program, would be to create one variable per title:
&lt;?php
$artigo1 = "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local";
$artigo2 = "Crítica: a nova exposição do museu";
$artigo3 = "Como nasceu a Contraponto";
This approach works while the magazine has three articles, but quickly becomes unsustainable: what if the magazine publishes fifty articles? What if an article is taken down, or a new one is published? Variables would need to be created, deleted or renamed one by one, and any code that needed to loop through “all the articles” would have to be rewritten every time the number of articles changed. This approach also lacks simple answers to everyday questions about a site like this: how many articles are published? What is the list of titles, in alphabetical order? None of these questions has an easy answer with separate variables.
The solution is an array: a data structure that allows several related values to be stored in a single variable, accessible through numeric indices or associative keys, instead of creating a separate variable for each value. PHP provides three types of arrays, each better suited to a different kind of problem, as the following sections show, always returning to the Contraponto site.

3.8.1 Indexed Arrays
The simplest type of array is the indexed (or numeric) array, in which each value is accessed through an integer index, which always starts at 0. It is the right structure for Contraponto’s titles problem, since there is no need to give each article its own name, only to store them all together:
&lt;?php
$titulos = array(
    "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local",
    "Crítica: a nova exposição do museu",
    "Como nasceu a Contraponto"
);

echo $titulos[0];  // Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local
echo $titulos[1];  // Crítica: a nova exposição do museu
echo $titulos[2];  // Como nasceu a Contraponto

$titulos[] = "Roteiro cultural de outubro";  // adds a new element at the end
echo count($titulos);                        // 4, the number of elements in the array
Two of Tiago’s questions are already solved with very little code: publishing a new article is a single statement, $titulos[] = "Roteiro cultural de outubro", with no need to declare any further variable; and knowing how many articles are published is just count($titulos), the number of elements in the array, which stays correct no matter how many articles are published or removed.
From PHP 5.4 onward, array("...", "...") can also be written with the shorthand syntax ["...", "..."], today the most common way to find it in recent PHP code.
Tiago’s third question is still unanswered: what is the list of articles, in alphabetical order? Besides count(), PHP provides several other functions for working with the content of an indexed array, without having to loop through its elements manually:
&lt;?php
$categorias = ["Tecnologia", "Cultura", "Música", "Cinema"];

array_push($categorias, "Entrevistas");   // adds to the end
$ultima = array_pop($categorias);         // removes the last element

sort($categorias);                        // sorts alphabetically
print_r($categorias);                     // [Cinema, Cultura, Música, Tecnologia]

if (in_array("Música", $categorias)) {
    echo "The Música category already exists";
}

$posicao = array_search("Cultura", $categorias);   // index of "Cultura"
echo $posicao;
Applied to the initial problem, sort($titulos) would reorder the article titles alphabetically, the same way this example sorts the categories, since PHP uses the same comparison mechanism for text and for numbers, only with different sorting criteria depending on the value’s type. The remaining functions also address common list-management needs: in_array() checks whether a category already exists before adding it again, and array_search() returns the position of a specific category, useful, for example, for removing it later.


3.8.2 Associative Arrays
An indexed array handles a simple list of titles well, but the Contraponto site will quickly need to store more information about each article, not just its title: the author, the category, the summary. Storing all this information in an indexed array would require memorising that index 0 always corresponds to the title, 1 to the author, and so on, code that is hard to read and easy to get wrong. An associative array solves exactly this problem: each value is accessed through a key of one’s choosing, typically a string, instead of a numeric index, which makes the code much more readable when each value has its own meaning:
&lt;?php
$artigo = array(
    "titulo"    =&gt; "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local",
    "autor"     =&gt; "Rita Ferreira",
    "categoria" =&gt; "Tecnologia"
);

echo $artigo["titulo"];   // Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local
echo $artigo["autor"];    // Rita Ferreira

// adds a new key
$artigo["resumo"] = "Uma conversa sobre os desafios do jornalismo de proximidade.";
A Contraponto article therefore stops being just a loose piece of text inside the $titulos array, and becomes, itself, an associative array with all its properties. This idea, an associative array inside another array, is precisely the topic of the next section.


3.8.3 Multidimensional Arrays
A multidimensional array is an array whose values are themselves other arrays, which makes it possible to represent more structured data, such as categories with lists of items, or a list of records, each with several fields — exactly the problem raised at the end of the previous section:
&lt;?php
$revistas_concorrentes = array(
    "generalistas" =&gt; array("Visão", "Sábado", "Expresso"),
    "culturais"    =&gt; array("Ler", "Blimunda", "Time Out")
);

echo $revistas_concorrentes["generalistas"][0];  // Visão
echo $revistas_concorrentes["culturais"][1];     // Blimunda

// An array of records, each one in turn an associative array:
// exactly the "Article" sketch Tiago proposed in @sec-03-project-en
$artigos = array(
    array(
        "titulo"    =&gt; "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local",
        "autor"     =&gt; "Rita Ferreira",
        "categoria" =&gt; "Tecnologia"
    ),
    array(
        "titulo"    =&gt; "Crítica: a nova exposição do museu",
        "autor"     =&gt; "Marco Antunes",
        "categoria" =&gt; "Cultura"
    ),
    array(
        "titulo"    =&gt; "Como nasceu a Contraponto",
        "autor"     =&gt; "Rita Ferreira",
        "categoria" =&gt; "Cultura"
    )
);

echo $artigos[0]["titulo"];  // Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local
echo $artigos[2]["autor"];   // Rita Ferreira
The $artigos array is the structure that finally gives shape to the model proposed in Section 3.2: each element is a complete article, with all its information, and the whole list can still be looped through, counted and sorted with the same functions seen in Section 3.8.1. It is also with this same array, in memory, that Tiago will build the site’s first pages, long before connecting the application to a real database; this last example, an array of associative arrays, is in fact the most common way to represent, in memory, a set of records coming from a database, a topic picked up in more detail in Section 8.1.
To quickly inspect the content of an array during development, without having to access each element individually, the print_r() function is used, which shows its full structure, including nested arrays, or var_dump(), which also shows the type and length of each value.


3.8.4 Complete Example: an Author’s Views
A small example, combining variables, types, conversion and multidimensional arrays, shows well how these concepts come together in practice: calculating the average views of an author’s latest articles, stored inside an associative array with their data:
&lt;?php
$autor = [
    "nome"   =&gt; "Rita Ferreira",
    "vistas" =&gt; [1200, 850, 430, 610]
];

1$soma = array_sum($autor["vistas"]);
2$numero_artigos = count($autor["vistas"]);
3$media = $soma / $numero_artigos;

echo $autor["nome"] . " tem uma média de " . $media . " vistas por artigo";

4if ($media &gt;= 500) {
    echo ", um alcance acima da média da revista.";
} else {
    echo ", um alcance abaixo da média da revista.";
}

1

Adds up the views of the latest articles: 1200 + 850 + 430 + 610 = 3090.

2

Counts how many articles were added up: 4.

3

Divides the sum by the number of articles, obtaining the average: 3090 / 4 = 772.5.

4

Compares the average with the magazine’s reference value, deciding the final result.


Note that array_sum() returns a float whenever the division is not exact, even when all the added-up views are integers, which is consistent with the dynamic-typing behaviour seen in Section 3.7: PHP automatically picks the most suitable type for the result of each operation.
The complete code from the in-class demonstration on variables and arrays, ready to copy, is available in Section B.2.

3.9 Challenge
Tiago now knows how to represent an article, and a list of articles, as arrays. Before moving on to the next chapter, the challenge is to put that knowledge to the test, completing Contraponto’s article catalogue, using only what has been covered in this chapter, with no loops or conditionals:

Complete the $artigos array with at least five articles, each with a title, author and category;
Display the title of the third article in the catalogue;
Build a $categorias array with the categories already used in the articles, with no repeats, and sort it alphabetically with sort();
Check, with in_array(), whether the category "Entrevistas" is already present in the $categorias array built in the previous point.

The starter file, with the $artigos array partially filled in, is available in Section C.2.

&lt;?php
$estado = "publicado";

if ($estado === "publicado") {
    echo "Mostrar o artigo aos leitores.";
} else {
    echo "Artigo ainda em rascunho, não mostrar.";
}

&lt;?php
$vistas = 850;

if ($vistas &gt;= 1000) {
    echo "Viral";
} elseif ($vistas &gt;= 500) {
    echo "Popular";
} elseif ($vistas &gt;= 100) {
    echo "Normal";
} else {
    echo "Pouco lido";
}

&lt;?php
$categoria = "Música";

switch ($categoria) {
    case "Cultura":
    case "Música":
        echo "Secção Lazer";
        break;
    case "Tecnologia":
        echo "Secção Inovação";
        break;
    default:
        echo "Outras notícias";
}

4.1.1 Comparison Operators
While testing the article-status if, Tiago runs into a detail of PHP that is worth understanding well before moving on. Conditions rest on comparison operators, which evaluate two values and always return a boolean, true or false:



Operator
Meaning




==
equal (compares only the value)


===
identical (compares the value and the type)


!=
not equal (compares only the value)


!==
not identical (compares the value and the type)


&lt;
less than


&gt;
greater than


&lt;=
less than or equal to


&gt;=
greater than or equal to



The distinction between == and === is one of the most common sources of subtle bugs in PHP, precisely because PHP automatically converts between types when using ==:
&lt;?php
var_dump(0 == "abc");    // false in most current PHP versions
var_dump(0 == "0");      // true: "0" is converted to the number 0
var_dump(0 === "0");     // false: different types, integer vs string
var_dump("10" == "1e1"); // true: both converted to the same number
var_dump("10" === "1e1");// false: they are different strings
For this reason, it is good practice to use === whenever the goal is to guarantee two values are really equal, both in value and in type, avoiding comparisons that rely on automatic conversions that are not always obvious. This distinction will become particularly relevant later in the book, once Tiago starts receiving data from real HTTP requests, in Section 7.5: an article identifier received through the URL always arrives as a string, even when it represents a number, and comparing that string against an id stored as an integer can have unexpected results if == is used instead of ===.


4.1.2 Logical Operators
Tiago realises that showing an article, in practice, depends on two conditions at once: the article has to be published, and its category cannot in the meantime have been archived. Testing this with two separate ifs would require nesting one inside the other; combining both conditions into one is the role of logical operators:

and (or &&): true only if both conditions are true;
or (or ||): true if at least one of the conditions is true;
xor: true if exactly one of the two conditions is true, but not both.

&lt;?php
$publicado = true;
$categoria_ativa = true;

if ($publicado && $categoria_ativa) {
    echo "Mostrar artigo.";
}

4.2 Loops
A loop is a control structure that allows a block of code to be executed repeatedly, until a specified condition is no longer met, or until a predetermined number of repetitions is reached. PHP provides four types of loops.

4.2.1 for
Used when the number of repetitions needed is known in advance, taking three parameters: the initial value, the stopping condition, and the increment applied at each repetition:
&lt;?php
for ($pagina = 1; $pagina &lt;= 5; $pagina++) {
    echo "Página $pagina ";
}
// Página 1 Página 2 Página 3 Página 4 Página 5


4.2.2 while
Takes only one condition, evaluated before each repetition; if the condition is false from the start, the code block never runs at all. In Contraponto’s management panel, an editor could use a while to work through, one by one, the comments still waiting for approval:
&lt;?php
$comentarios_pendentes = 5;
while ($comentarios_pendentes &gt; 0) {
    echo "Comentário pendente: $comentarios_pendentes ";
    $comentarios_pendentes--;
}


4.2.3 do...while
Very similar to while, but with the condition evaluated after each repetition, which guarantees the code block always runs at least once, even if the condition is false from the start. It is useful, for example, to guarantee the home page always shows some featured article, even on days when none was explicitly marked as such:
&lt;?php
$artigos_destaque = 0;   // no article marked as featured today
do {
    echo "A mostrar o artigo mais recente como destaque. ";
    $artigos_destaque--;
} while ($artigos_destaque &gt; 0);
// runs once, even though the condition is already false from the start


4.2.4 foreach
Used specifically to loop through arrays, with no need to manage an index manually. It is finally with a foreach that Tiago manages to turn the $titulos array, sketched in Section 3.8.1, into a real list of articles on the site’s page, without repeating echo once per article:
&lt;?php
$titulos = [
    "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local",
    "Crítica: a nova exposição do museu",
    "Como nasceu a Contraponto"
];

foreach ($titulos as $titulo) {
    echo $titulo . "&lt;br&gt;";
}
With an associative array, foreach allows both the key and the value of each element to be accessed at the same time, which is useful, for example, for showing every field of a single article without writing a separate echo for each property:
&lt;?php
$artigo = [
    "titulo"    =&gt; "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local",
    "autor"     =&gt; "Rita Ferreira",
    "categoria" =&gt; "Tecnologia"
];

foreach ($artigo as $chave =&gt; $valor) {
    echo "$chave: $valor&lt;br&gt;";
}

4.3 Challenge
With conditionals and loops, Tiago can now turn the static article catalogue into a page that reacts to the data. The challenge, building on the $artigos catalogue completed in Section 3.9, is:

Use a foreach to loop through $artigos and display the title of each one;
Inside the same foreach, use an if to also display the author, but only for articles whose category is "Cultura";
Define, at the top of the file, a variable $categoria_escolhida = "Tecnologia";, standing in, for now, for the category the reader wants to see, and use an if so the foreach only shows articles from that category.

The starter file, already with the previous chapter’s complete catalogue, is available in Section C.3. This fixed variable is, deliberately, a simplification: Section 7.5 shows how to replace it with the real value from a browser request, through $_GET.

&lt;?php
function formatarAutoria($autor, $categoria) {
    return "por " . $autor . ", em " . $categoria;
}

echo formatarAutoria("Rita Ferreira", "Tecnologia");
echo formatarAutoria("Marco Antunes", "Cultura");

&lt;?php
function tempoLeitura($palavras, $palavras_por_minuto = 200) {
    return ceil($palavras / $palavras_por_minuto);
}

echo tempoLeitura(600);       // 3, uses the default value (200 words/minute)
echo tempoLeitura(600, 300);  // 2, the given value overrides the default

&lt;?php
function mostrarTeaser($titulo, $resumo) {
    echo "&lt;h3&gt;" . $titulo . "&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;" . $resumo . "&lt;/p&gt;";
}

mostrarTeaser(
    "Entrevista: o futuro do jornalismo local",
    "Uma conversa sobre os desafios do jornalismo de proximidade."
);
mostrarTeaser(
    "Como nasceu a Contraponto",
    "A história da revista, contada pela sua diretora."
);

&lt;?php
function formatarVistas($vistas) {
    $sufixo = "vistas";     // $sufixo only exists inside this function
    if ($vistas === 1) {
        $sufixo = "vista";
    }
    return $vistas . " " . $sufixo;
}

echo formatarVistas(850);  // 850 vistas
echo formatarVistas(1);    // 1 vista
// echo $sufixo;           // error: $sufixo is not defined here, outside the function

5.2 Dates
The article draft proposed in Section 3.2 includes a publication date, a field none of the previous examples has used yet. Tiago needs to display it legibly to the reader, to calculate how long ago an article was published, and to let Rita schedule an article for a future date. PHP offers functions dedicated to handling dates and times for this kind of need. There are three common operations.

5.2.1 Getting the Current Date and Time
The date() function formats the server’s current date and time according to a pattern given as a parameter, using letters with predefined meanings, such as d for the day, m for the month, Y for the four-digit year, H for the hour, i for the minutes and s for the seconds. This is the function Tiago uses to record the date when a new article is created:
&lt;?php
echo date("d/m/Y");        // 24/08/2026
echo date("d/m/Y H:i:s");  // 24/08/2026 15:42:07
echo date("l, d \d\e F");  // segunda-feira, 24 de Agosto (localised)


5.2.2 Custom Formatting
As shown in the previous examples, the same instant can be presented in very different ways, simply by changing the format given to date(), which is useful for adapting how the publication date is shown to the context: a compact format in the article listing, or a written-out format in the header of a single article’s page.


5.2.3 Time Zones
By default, PHP uses the time zone configured on the server, which may not match the time zone of Contraponto’s readers. If the server is hosted outside Portugal, the publication date shown could come out wrong without anyone realising why; to avoid this, the time zone to use is set explicitly with date_default_timezone_set(), right at the start of the script:
&lt;?php
date_default_timezone_set("Europe/Lisbon");
echo date("H:i:s");  // local time in Portugal, regardless of the server's time zone
For more complex operations on dates, PHP provides the object-oriented DateTime class, already mentioned in Section 3.4. Unlike date(), which receives everything as function parameters, here an object representing the date itself is created first, with new DateTime(...), and its methods are then called through an arrow (-&gt;) to query or modify it, such as -&gt;modify() or -&gt;format(). It is with this tool that Tiago solves two concrete Contraponto problems: showing how many days ago an article was published, and scheduling the publication of an article for a future date:
&lt;?php
$data_publicacao = new DateTime("2026-08-20");
$hoje = new DateTime();
$diferenca = $data_publicacao-&gt;diff($hoje);
echo $diferenca-&gt;days . " dias desde a publicação";  // e.g.: 4 dias

$data_agendada = new DateTime();
$data_agendada-&gt;modify("+7 days");
echo "Publicação agendada para " . $data_agendada-&gt;format("d/m/Y");
The complete code for the demonstration done in class about dates, including the leap-year calculation, is available in Section B.5.

5.3 Controlling Page Flow: Includes
The Contraponto site will have several pages, the article listing, an article’s page, the comment form, but they all share the same header, with the logo and the category menu, and the same footer, with the copyright notice. Copying that HTML into every new page would be just as fragile as repeating a function’s logic: if Rita asks for the menu to change, Tiago would have to remember to update every page, one by one. Instead of repeating that code in every file, PHP allows the content of one file to be included inside another, through two very similar instructions: include and require.
include: includes the content of a file inside the current file. If the given file is not found, PHP issues a warning, but the script keeps running. It is useful when including optional files, not critical to the script’s operation.
require: works similarly to include, but is used when the included file is essential to the script’s operation. If the given file is not found, PHP issues a fatal error and immediately stops execution. It is useful when it is necessary to guarantee that a fundamental file is available before continuing to run the rest of the script.
&lt;?php
require "header.php";
?&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Últimos Artigos&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Aqui aparece a listagem de artigos da Contraponto.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;?php
require "footer.php";
?&gt;
Since the header and footer are usually essential to a page’s visual structure, without which the result would look incomplete or broken, it is common to prefer require over include to include them. There are also the include_once and require_once variants, which guarantee that the same file is only included once, even if the instruction appears, directly or indirectly, more than once in the execution flow, avoiding errors such as redefining the same function.
This pattern, header.php and footer.php files included in every page of the site, is the first practical step towards thinking of a web application as a set of reusable components, rather than independent HTML pages disconnected from one another, an idea revisited in more depth in Section 2.1.2 and in Section 10.1.
The complete code for the three files in this demonstration is available in Section B.6.

5.4 Challenge
With functions, dates and includes, Tiago now has the pieces to organise the site’s code instead of repeating it. The challenge, starting from the $artigos catalogue and the category filter from Section 4.3, is:

Write a function formatarArtigo($artigo) that receives an article (an element of the $artigos array) and returns a string with the title and the formatted authorship, for example "Título — por Autor";
Use that function inside the foreach that already iterates over $artigos, instead of writing the formatting directly inside it;
Create two files, header.php and footer.php, with a simple header and footer for the site, and use require to include them before and after the article listing.

The starter file, with the catalogue and the category filter already in place, is available in Section C.4.

finding the address of the magazine’s server;
determining the path the data must travel to get there;
establishing a reliable connection;
converting the data into electrical signals or radio pulses.















Figure 6.1: Protocols (horizontal arrows) connect matching layers on different devices; services (vertical arrows) connect adjacent layers within the same device.

A presentation language: HTML;
An addressing scheme: URI/URL;
A communication protocol between client and server: HTTP.

7.3 What Happens When a URL Is Typed
It is worth following, step by step, what happens between the moment a Contraponto reader types contraponto.pt into the browser and the moment the magazine’s home page appears on screen, tying together everything seen in the previous chapter:

The browser extracts the server name (hostname) from the URL, including the port number, if given, and queries DNS to obtain its IP address;
The browser then opens a TCP socket, specifying the destination IP and the port to use (by default, port 80 for a typical web page);
It writes an HTTP request message and sends it through that TCP socket; if no web page name is explicitly given in the URL, the server assumes index.html by default (if this does not exist, it looks for other default pages, such as index.php, default.html, etc.);
The TCP layer accepts the message and guarantees its reliable delivery to the server, as described in Section 6.3.1;
The server responds, sending a response message that travels back along the same protocol stack, until it reaches the browser again, which interprets it and presents the page to the user.

7.5 Superglobals
One piece is still missing to close the loop: how does the artigos.php script from the previous example actually get the value 5 of the id parameter? For this, PHP automatically provides a set of predefined arrays, known as superglobals: variables automatically initialised by PHP, available anywhere in a script, regardless of the context or the function it is in, with no need to declare them. It is through these that PHP code has access to everything seen in this chapter so far: the parameters of a GET request, the fields of a POST form, cookies, the session. The most used are:

$_GET: contains the data sent through the URL, exactly the id parameter of artigos.php?id=5, or the fields of a form submitted with method="GET", as seen in Section 7.4.1;
$_POST: contains the data sent in the body of a POST request, typically by a form such as the comment one seen in Section 7.4.2;
$_REQUEST: combines the data from $_GET, $_POST and the request’s cookies, useful when it does not matter by which method the data arrived;
$_SESSION: stores the user’s session information, a topic covered in more depth later in this chapter, in Section 7.9.2;
$_COOKIE: contains the cookies sent by the client to the server, a topic covered in more depth in Section 7.9.1;
$_SERVER: contains information about the server and about the current request, such as the client’s address, the HTTP method used, or the script’s own path.

On the Contraponto site, $_GET is precisely the mechanism that lets a reader filter articles by category, through an address such as contraponto.pt/artigos.php?categoria=Cultura, finally replacing, with real data from a request, the fixed variable used in Section 4.3:
&lt;?php
// contraponto.pt/artigos.php?categoria=Cultura
if (isset($_GET["categoria"])) {
    echo "A mostrar artigos da categoria: " . $_GET["categoria"];
} else {
    echo "A mostrar todos os artigos.";
}
The use of isset() in the previous example is not accidental: directly accessing a $_GET or $_POST key that does not exist generates a warning in PHP, so it is good practice to always check whether a given field was actually sent before using it, since not every reader arrives at the site through an address with that parameter.
$_POST, in turn, is what receives the comment form sketched in Section 3.2, when a reader submits it:
&lt;?php
if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_METHOD"] === "POST") {
    $nome_comentario = $_POST["nome"];
    $texto_comentario = $_POST["texto"];
    echo "Comentário de $nome_comentario recebido: $texto_comentario";
}
Superglobals are the connecting point between HTTP, on the network side, and the PHP logic, on the server side: the category filter and the comment form only take effect because their data reaches the PHP script through $_GET or $_POST. The complete code for this demonstration, including the HTML form used to test $_GET and $_POST, is available in Section B.3.

7.7 The Server’s Response
The server’s response follows a structure similar to the request’s, but starts with a status line, instead of a method and a URL:
[version] [status code] [reason phrase]
[headers]

[body]
The status code is a number summarising the outcome of the request, grouped into five categories:

1xx: informational, rarely used directly by web application programmers;
2xx: success, such as 200 OK;
3xx: redirection, such as 301 Moved Permanently, indicating that the requested resource is now available at another address;
4xx: client-side error, such as 404 Not Found, when the requested resource does not exist, or 403 Forbidden, when access is denied;
5xx: server-side error, such as 500 Internal Server Error, indicating a failure in the server-side application, typically a bug in the PHP code itself.

