9.3 Best Practices
A few simple practices significantly reduce the security risk of a web application:

Regular updates: keep the underlying software (PHP itself, the web server, the database management system) and all third-party dependencies and extensions updated to the latest version, since many vulnerabilities are only exploited after they become publicly known;
Principle of least privilege: every component of an application, and every database user, should have only the permissions strictly necessary for its function, never full access for convenience;
Data validation and sanitisation: always treat any data coming from outside the application with suspicion, validating its format and sanitising its content before using it, whether in a database query or on a page shown to another user.

10.2 Rebuilding the CRUD with MVC
Applying MVC to Contraponto’s small article-management application, built in Section 8.9, means reorganising the code, keeping the same behaviour, but splitting it into files with well-defined responsibilities:

Model (Artigo.php): concentrates all the database access logic related to articles, with functions such as listarArtigos(), criarArtigo($titulo, $autor, $categoria), atualizarArtigo($id, $titulo, $autor, $categoria) and apagarArtigo($id). None of these functions produce HTML; they only return data.
View (views/lista.php, views/formulario.php): concentrate only HTML, with small snippets of PHP to display the data provided to them, with no direct access to the database.
Controller (ArtigoController.php): receives Rita’s request, decides which Model function to call depending on the requested action — listing, creating, editing or deleting — and chooses the appropriate View to display the result.

&lt;?php
// ArtigoController.php (excerpt)
require "Artigo.php";

function acaoListar() {
    $artigos = listarArtigos();
    require "views/lista.php";  // the View uses $artigos
}

function acaoCriar() {
    if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_METHOD"] === "POST") {
        criarArtigo($_POST["titulo"], $_POST["autor"], $_POST["categoria"]);
        header("Location: index.php");
        exit;
    }
    require "views/formulario.php";
}
The final behaviour, for whoever uses the application, is the same as before. What changed was entirely internal: whoever changes the visual appearance only touches the Views, whoever changes the business rules only touches the Model, and the Controller connects the two parts without depending on the internal details of either.

10.3 Business Rules in the Model
The Model is not limited to running SQL queries: it is also the natural place for the application’s business rules, the logic that determines whether an operation is valid, regardless of how the data arrived there. Consider validating the category before creating a new article, using in_array(), already seen in Section 3.8.1:
&lt;?php
// Artigo.php (excerpt)
function criarArtigo($titulo, $autor, $categoria) {
    $categorias_validas = ["Cultura", "Tecnologia", "Música"];
    if (!in_array($categoria, $categorias_validas)) {
        return ["sucesso" =&gt; false, "erro" =&gt; "Categoria inválida"];
    }

    global $conexao;
    $stmt = $conexao-&gt;prepare("INSERT INTO artigos (titulo, autor, categoria) VALUES (?, ?, ?)");
    $stmt-&gt;bind_param("sss", $titulo, $autor, $categoria);
    $stmt-&gt;execute();

    return ["sucesso" =&gt; true];
}
This validation belongs in the Model, not in the Controller or the View, because it is a rule about the data itself — “an article must belong to one of the magazine’s categories” — that applies whenever an article is created, regardless of whether the request came from a web form, a future API, or a batch import script. Placing this check in the Controller, by contrast, would require repeating it in every new entry point that came to create articles.

10.4 The Front Controller
The original mini application had one PHP file per action: index.php, add.php, edit.php, delete.php. A common alternative, especially as the number of actions grows, is the front controller pattern: instead of several entry points, there is a single file, usually also called index.php, which receives every request and decides, based on a parameter, which Controller action to run:
&lt;?php
// index.php as a front controller
require "ArtigoController.php";

$acao = $_GET["acao"] ?? "listar";

switch ($acao) {
    case "criar":
        acaoCriar();
        break;
    case "editar":
        acaoEditar();
        break;
    default:
        acaoListar();
}
This pattern centralises decisions that would otherwise have to be repeated in every file, such as checking whether the user has an active session before allowing any action. It is also the mechanism most web development frameworks, the topic of the next chapter, use behind their routing system.

Routing system: instead of the manual switch built in Section 10.4, a framework allows routes to be declared directly, associating an address with a Controller function;
ORM (Object-Relational Mapping): instead of writing SQL by hand with mysqli, as in Section 8.7, an ORM represents each table as a PHP class, and each row as an object, allowing interaction with the database through PHP code instead of direct SQL statements;
Template engine: instead of mixing PHP and HTML directly in the Views, a template engine offers a cleaner syntax for generating dynamic HTML, with features such as layout inheritance, to avoid repeating the header and footer on every page;
Migrations: files that describe, in code, the database structure, allowing that structure to be created, changed and shared between programmers without relying on CREATE TABLE statements written manually by each one.

11.2 Laravel
Laravel includes, among others, Eloquent, its native ORM, and Blade, its template engine. A query that, with mysqli, would require writing the SQL statement explicitly, is written, in Eloquent, as a method call on a class that represents the table:
// With mysqli (chapter 8)
$stmt = $conexao-&gt;prepare("SELECT * FROM artigos WHERE id = ?");
$stmt-&gt;bind_param("i", $id);
$stmt-&gt;execute();

// With Eloquent (Laravel)
$artigo = Artigo::find($id);
Laravel’s routing system directly replaces the manual switch in the front controller built in Section 10.4, associating an address with a function declaratively:
// routes/web.php
Route::get('/articles', [ArticleController::class, 'index']);
Route::post('/articles', [ArticleController::class, 'store']);
Route::get('/articles/{id}/edit', [ArticleController::class, 'edit']);
Migrations, in turn, replace the CREATE TABLE statements written by hand in Section 8.5, describing the table structure in PHP code, versionable and shareable across the whole team:
// database/migrations/create_articles_table.php
Schema::create('articles', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table-&gt;id();                    // INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY
    $table-&gt;string('title');
    $table-&gt;string('author');
    $table-&gt;string('category');
    $table-&gt;timestamps();            // automatic creation/update timestamps
});
Laravel also automatically generates much of the folder structure that was built manually throughout this course: an app/Models folder for the Model classes, a resources/views folder for the Blade Views, and a routes folder to declare the application’s routes. The initial learning curve of a framework such as Laravel is steeper than writing plain PHP, because it requires understanding its own conventions, but it pays off quickly as an application grows, preventing every team from reinventing, in its own way, the same mechanisms for database connections, authentication or page generation.

A.5 Verifying the Installation
Whatever option is chosen, and whether the environment is provided by the lecturer or built from scratch, the final test is the same: confirming that PHP can actually communicate with MySQL. In the folder served by Apache (/var/www/html in the virtual machine, or src in the containers), create a small test file:
&lt;?php
$connection = mysqli_connect("localhost", "root", "root");

if ($connection) {
    echo "Successfully connected to MySQL!";
} else {
    echo "Connection error: " . mysqli_connect_error();
}
Opening this file in the browser should show the success message. If, instead, an error message appears, the most common reasons are that MySQL has not fully started yet, the password given does not match the one set during installation, or, in the case of containers, the server name is not localhost but rather the service name defined in docker-compose.yml (db, in the example above), since, from inside a container, localhost refers to the container itself, not to other containers in the same application.
If phpMyAdmin is available, referred to in Section A.1.4, this same test can be done visually, without writing any code: simply authenticate with the MySQL credentials and confirm that the created database appears in the list.
With this test successful, the environment is ready for the PHP development work resumed in Section 3.4.

B.2 Demo: Variables and Arrays
Demonstration used in Section 3.6 and Section 3.8, with examples of the four array types seen in that chapter: numeric, associative and multidimensional.
&lt;?php
// Declare variables of different types
$age = 19;                    // Integer
$lunchPrice = 10.5;           // Float
$text = "Hello, World!";      // String
$doorOpen = true;             // Boolean
$simpleArray = array(1, 2, 3);  // Array

// Show the values in a readable way
echo "&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Age:&lt;/strong&gt; $age&lt;/p&gt;";
echo "&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lunch price:&lt;/strong&gt; $lunchPrice&lt;/p&gt;";
echo "&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Text:&lt;/strong&gt; $text&lt;/p&gt;";

// echo of a boolean shows "1" for true and nothing for false;
// to show readable text, a ternary expression is used
echo "&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Door open:&lt;/strong&gt; " . ($doorOpen ? 'true' : 'false') . "&lt;/p&gt;";

// echoing an array directly produces a Warning; use print_r() or var_dump()
echo "&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Simple array:&lt;/strong&gt; ";
print_r($simpleArray);
echo "&lt;/p&gt;";

// Indexed array
$fruits = array("Apple", "Banana", "Orange");
echo "&lt;p&gt;First fruit: " . $fruits[0] . "&lt;/p&gt;";
echo "&lt;p&gt;Second fruit: " . $fruits[1] . "&lt;/p&gt;";
echo "&lt;p&gt;Third fruit: " . $fruits[2] . "&lt;/p&gt;";

// Associative array
$person = array(
    "name" =&gt; "John",
    "age"  =&gt; 25,
    "city" =&gt; "Lisbon"
);
echo "&lt;p&gt;Name: " . $person["name"] . "&lt;/p&gt;";
echo "&lt;p&gt;Age: " . $person["age"] . "&lt;/p&gt;";
echo "&lt;p&gt;City: " . $person["city"] . "&lt;/p&gt;";

// Multidimensional array
$cars = array(
    "sports" =&gt; array("Ferrari", "Lamborghini", "Porsche"),
    "suv"    =&gt; array("BMW X5", "Audi Q7", "Mercedes GLE"),
