D.4 The Structure of the Headers
Section 6.3 explains that each layer adds its own control information to the data, in a process called encapsulation, and summarises what each one adds: TCP the ports, IP the addresses, MAC the physical addresses. It is worth seeing, in detail, the exact structure of each of these headers, field by field.

D.4.1 TCP
The TCP header includes, besides the source and destination ports, the sequence number and the acknowledgement number:






Figure D.3: The field-by-field structure of a TCP segment.




Flags: six single-bit indicators, which signal the role of a segment in the communication. SYN starts a new connection, FIN ends it in an orderly way, ACK signals that the acknowledgement field is valid, RST abruptly interrupts the connection (typically due to an error), PSH asks that the data be delivered immediately to the application, without waiting to group it with others, and URG signals urgent data;
Window (Window Size): the size of the flow-control window. A window is a set of segments that can be sent all at once, for example 100 segments, without waiting for an individual acknowledgement of each one before sending the next. If the receiver takes too long to acknowledge receipt, or if the network starts showing signs of congestion, the window size is automatically reduced, decreasing the sending rate; when the network is free again, the window size increases again. In this way, TCP adjusts itself to the network conditions at each moment, with no need for the application using it to do anything;
Checksum: a value calculated from the content of the segment, which lets the receiver detect whether the data was corrupted during transmission;
Options: a variable-length field, used by extensions to the TCP protocol not provided for in the basic header.



D.4.2 IP
The IP header includes, besides the source and destination addresses:






Figure D.4: The field-by-field structure of an IP packet.




Version: the IP protocol in use (4 or 6);
IHL (Internet Header Length): the length of the header itself, since it can vary depending on the options present;
Total length of the packet;
Identification: a number used to associate with one another all the fragments resulting from the same original packet, when it is fragmented;
Hop count (TTL, Time To Live): the maximum number of hops (passes through a router) a packet can take before being discarded, a safety mechanism to prevent a misrouted packet from circulating indefinitely across the network;
Fragmentation: each network has a maximum packet size it can carry (the MTU, Maximum Transmission Unit); if an IP packet exceeds that size, it is fragmented into smaller parts, a process carried out by intermediate nodes on the network;
Protocol: indicates whether the content of the packet, in its data section, is a TCP segment or a UDP datagram, informing the transport layer, on the receiving side, how to interpret what follows.



D.4.3 Ethernet
A complete Ethernet frame, under the IEEE 802.3 standard, has the following structure:






Figure D.5: The structure of an Ethernet frame, under the IEEE 802.3 standard.



Besides the destination (DA) and source (SA) addresses, the frame includes a preamble, a fixed sequence of bits that lets receiving network interfaces synchronise with the start of the transmission; a length field, with the size of the data carried; an FCS (Frame Check Sequence), similar in purpose to TCP’s checksum, which allows corrupted frames to be detected; and an interframe gap, a small mandatory pause before the next frame can be transmitted.

E.2 The Exact Syntax of HTTP Messages
Section 7.4 presents the generic structure of an HTTP message. It is worth seeing, in detail, what each of its parts contains in the concrete case of a GET or POST request.

E.2.1 The GET Request
The link to a Contraponto category’s articles, seen in Section 7.4.1,
&lt;a href="contraponto.pt/artigos.php?categoria=Cultura"&gt;Cultura&lt;/a&gt;
when clicked, results in the following GET message:
GET /artigos.php?categoria=Cultura HTTP/1.1
Host: contraponto.pt
GET is the method, /artigos.php?categoria=Cultura is the requested resource, and HTTP/1.1 is the protocol version in use. The Host header indicates which server the request is intended for, essential when the same IP address hosts several different domains. The GET message has no body, because there is no need to send additional data to the server, only to request a resource.
Because it changes nothing on the server, the GET message is considered safe. This is a convention of the protocol, not a technical guarantee: a poorly designed server can, technically, change data in response to a GET, but doing so violates the behaviour expected by any HTTP client, and is considered bad practice.
GET can also be used in an HTML form context, as long as the goal is not to change information on the server, only to obtain a result, as in a search:
&lt;form action="/pesquisa" method="GET"&gt;
    &lt;label for="term"&gt;Procurar:&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="term" name="term" type="text" /&gt;
    &lt;input type="submit" value="Procurar!" /&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
When this form is submitted with the term “jornalismo”, the browser generates the following message:
GET /pesquisa?term=jornalismo HTTP/1.1
Host: contraponto.pt
Note how the form data appears directly in the URL, after the ?, in the key=value format, exactly as in the category link.


E.2.2 The POST Request
Contraponto’s comment form, referred to in Section 7.4.2, typically has this form:
&lt;form action="/comentar.php" method="POST"&gt;
    &lt;label for="nome"&gt;Nome&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="nome" name="nome" type="text" /&gt;
    &lt;label for="texto"&gt;Comentário&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;textarea id="texto" name="texto"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;
    &lt;input type="submit" value="Comentar" /&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
Which, when submitted, results in the following message:
POST /comentar.php HTTP/1.1
Host: contraponto.pt

nome=Rita&texto=Excelente+artigo%21
Unlike GET, the data goes in the body of the message, not in the URL. Notice how the key=value format is preserved, but now in the body, and how special characters in the text (such as !) appear encoded (%21), a rule called URL encoding, necessary because the body of an HTTP message can only contain a restricted set of characters.

E.4 Browser Connection Management
Section 7.8 explains that each HTTP request is, in itself, independent of all the others, and that a single page normally requires several requests, one for each resource. To ease this cost, browsers rely on different connection-management strategies:

Parallel connections: on detecting several resources on a page, the browser opens several connections at once to load them faster, one for the HTML, another for the CSS, another for the JavaScript, and so on. The number of parallel connections is limited, because each additional connection increases the load on the server, which, beyond a certain point, can make the page load more slowly instead of faster;
Persistent connections: instead of closing the socket right after each response, the server keeps it open for several successive requests. Apache, by default, keeps a persistent connection open for 5 seconds of inactivity before closing it. This period also has to be limited for security reasons, to prevent denial-of-service attacks (DoS), in which an attacker deliberately keeps many connections open at once, preventing the server from handling legitimate requests;
Back-to-back connections: since HTTP/1.1, a client can send several requests in a row, over the same connection, without waiting for the response to each one before sending the next, further reducing the total load time of a page with many resources.
